Z||000012||MinnGreenGT||11-14-2001||01:55 PM||||Here's an excerpt from a Brief article to explain:<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Britain's Rather Silly Christmas Tradition<P><BR>By STEVE LOHR<BR>Special to The New York Times<BR>25xii87<BR>NORWICH, England, Dec. 23<P>Nothing does more for the worldwide reputation of English eccentricity than this<BR>nation's Christmastime penchant for crackers.<P>No, not the things you put cheese on and eat - those are biscuits, in native<BR>English. Here, crackers are cylinders wrapped in crepe paper that snap loudly<BR>when pulled apart and contain small gifts, silly hats and fortune cookie-style<BR>jokes that range from the merely bad to the truly dreadful.<P>To the uninitiated, English crackers seem more suited to a New Year's Eve<BR>celebration than to Christmas. But there will be a cracker or two at virtually<BR>every<BR>Christmas-dinner place setting in Britain this year, as there have been for<BR>decades. In. fact, more than 120 million crackers will be snapped this Christmas<BR>in Britain, or 2.1 crackers for every man, woman and child in the United<BR>Kingdom.<P>Many of them will contain "real groaners" that are deemed the height of<BR>cracker humor. Every year, London newspapers review the year's cracker-joke<BR>trends and repeat some that rate high on the "groanometer" scale. A few of this<BR>year's crop:<P>What is a skeleton called when it doesn't get up in the morning? Lazy bones,<BR>Where can you find health, wealth and happiness? In a dictionary.<BR>What do you call a monster with no luck? A luck-ness monster.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||1
